nd matched budgets where applicable. Defense Baselines. We compare DRG with direct low-frequency suppression (LF-Suppression), Deep Feature Loss (DFL) speech denoising ( Germain, Chen, and Koltun 2018 ) , and MMSE-STSA enhancement ( Ephraim and Malah 1984 ) . Metrics and Evaluation Protocol. We report accuracy for MMAU and RAVDESS. LibriSpeech uses WER, whereas CoVoST 2 uses BLEU ( Papineni et al. 2002 ) . We assess objective stealthiness with the audible noise ratio defined in Section Acoustic Stealthiness ; lower values indicate that less perturbation energy falls in the nominal audible band. Subjective stealthiness uses human ratings from 1 to 7, where lower values indicate lower perceived audibility. Defense detection uses precision, recall, and F1, while recovery uses the corresponding end-task metric after requery. Attack Effectiveness Figure 2: Attack effectiveness on translation and recognition. Lower BLEU and higher WER indicate stronger disruption. Table 1 and Figure 2 show that the fixed ILL waveform degrades all four task types. This effect does not weaken after black-box transfer: the largest RAVDESS reduction, 67 percentage points, occurs on the unseen StepAudio2 target rather than on the Qwen2.5 reference model. The consistent degradation on the other five LALMs therefore indicates that ILL has strong migration capabilities. ILL does not uniformly outperform every audible-noise baseline, but it achieves comparable and, in some settings, stronger disruption using only the 5–20 Hz band. It gives the lowest CoVoST 2 BLEU on every target in Figure 2 and remains competitive on the accuracy-based tasks. The key finding is therefore not uniform numerical dominance, but that a low-perceptibility frequency band alone can cause broad degradation across LALMs. Acoustic Stealthiness Table 2 reports the proportion of perturbation energy that falls in the measured audible band. Human hearing is conventionally characterized over approximately 20 Hz–20 kHz ( Salt and Hullar 2010 ) . Because ILL operates at 5–20 Hz, we quantify this spectral leakage as: ANR = 100 × E noise ​ ( 20 ​ Hz ​ – ​ 8 ​ kHz ) E noise ​ ( full band ) , \mathrm{ANR}=100	imes rac{E_{\mathrm{noise}}(20\,\mathrm{Hz}	ext{--}8\,\mathrm{kHz})}{E_{\mathrm{noise}}(	ext{full band})}, (13) where lower ANR indicates less perturbation energy in this band. Across the six datasets, ILL has an ANR of only 0.06–0.08%, whereas all evaluated baselines exceed 98.9%. ILL therefore achieves its task-level effect with negligible spectral leakage into the measured audible band. Figure 3: Human audibility ratings across 100+ responses. We complement ANR with 112 complete human response sets covering clean audio and all seven interference methods. Figure 3 shows that ILL receives a mean rating of 1.33 and a median of 1, close to the clean condition with a mean of 1.17 and a median of 1. Every comparison method receives a higher mean rating; the closest are Audio-Adv. at 3.75 and Whisper at 3.92. The agreement between the human ratings and ANR supports the low perceptibility of ILL in this evaluation without treating spectral leakage alone as evidence of human imperceptibility. Defense Evaluation Table 3: Precision, recall, and F1 scores (%) for DRG interference detection under different detector training datasets. Training dataset Metric MMAU LibriSpeech CoVoST2 RAVDESS LibriSpeech Precision 93.40 93.46 88.50 59.17 Recall 99.00 100.00 100.00 100.00 F1-score 96.12 96.62 93.90 74.35 CoVoST2 Precision 88.24 85.19 86.41 60.24 Recall 88.24 85.19 86.41 60.24 F1-score 89.11 88.46 87.68 75.19 Average Precision 99.00 97.98 100.00 81.30 Recall 99.00 97.00 97.00 100.00 F1-score 99.00 97.49 98.48 89.69 DRG first identifies whether the input contains low-frequency interference. As shown in Table 3 , the Average training condition yields F1 scores of 89.69–99.00% across the four evaluation datasets. The result indicates that the spectral distribution provides a reliable trigger for conditional 